maria: Heads-up — running into flaky client-side SSE parsing when chunks split JSON tokens. Seen in both the node-fetch sample app and our browser SDK tests. Repro steps below, attaching logs.

sam: Ugh. Is this the case where a single logical "data:" line gets split across TCP frames?

maria: Yep. Example from local test (server emits a JSON object but the socket sends it in two chunks):

```
SSE stream bytes chunk 1: "data: {\"id\":1, \"text\": \"Hel\"\n"
SSE stream bytes chunk 2: "lo\"}\n
client parser (simplified):
accumulator += chunk;
if (accumulator.includes('\n\n')) parse(accumulator); // naive newline-only parse
```

maria: The naive client waits for a full \n\n delim and then tries to JSON.parse, but because the server emitted a split inside a multibyte sequence in some builds, parse throws or the client times out waiting for newline. Browser SDK tests often just hang until our 30s polling timeout.

josh: Are we seeing any proxy in the middle? nginx buffering can coalesce or split differently. Also HTTP/2 vs HTTP/1.1 matters.

maria: In one failing environment it was our staging nginx with proxy_buffering on. But I also saw a failure with direct dedicated runtime -> browser when network dropped mid-chunk (client disconnect resumed). So multiple causes.

eva: I made a quick test harness and reproduced with node client by forcing small write buffer sizes, confirmed a split inside the JSON token. Also saw errors like `SyntaxError: Unexpected end of JSON input` and then the client retry logic does a full reconnect which duplicates response content.

sam: Two orthogonal fixes we can do: server-side — ensure each SSE `data:` line is flushed atomically with \n\n and avoid splitting JSON across writes; or client-side — parse incrementally (streaming JSON parser) and accept partial tokens without throwing, plus detect keepalive gaps. I lean to make client resilient and still harden server.

maria: Agreed. Quick wins I'd propose:
- client: buffer and only JSON.parse complete objects; use a small streaming parser fallback; treat incomplete JSON as temporary and wait for next chunk with a short backoff (200ms) before giving up
- client: add strict duplicate suppression for reconnects (idempotency keys)
- server: emit keepalive comments `: keepalive\n\n` every 10s so clients don't assume dead
- nginx: set `proxy_buffering off; chunked_transfer_encoding on;` for our SSE endpoints

josh: I tested `proxy_buffering off` + `proxy_request_buffering off` on a staging box and the split problem reduced dramatically. Also set `sendfile off` for the proxy in our config to avoid unexpected chunk coalescing. Results: 0 failures after 30m of stress (was ~5-10/min).

sam: SDK tasks:
- implement streaming-json fallback in node/browser SDKs (small incremental parser)
- expose a `sseKeepaliveInterval` option default 10s
- add `sseRetryDebounceMs` to avoid immediate reconnect storms when parse fails

maria: I'll open the PR for docs and examples showing the recommended client pattern. Short snippet to show robust client approach:

```js
let buf = '';
fetch(url, { /* ... */ }).then(r => {
  const reader = r.body.getReader();
  function pump() {
    return reader.read().then(({done, value}) => {
      if (done) return;
      buf += decoder.decode(value, {stream: true});
      // split on SSE \n\n boundaries
      let parts = buf.split('\n\n');
      buf = parts.pop(); // last piece may be partial
      for (const p of parts) {
        // p starts with "data:" possibly multiple lines
        const lines = p.split('\n').filter(Boolean);
        for (const l of lines) {
          if (l.startsWith('data:')) {
            try { const payload = JSON.parse(l.replace(/^data:\s*/, '')); handle(payload); }
            catch (e) { /* buffer will absorb next chunk, don't throw */ }
          }
        }
      }
      return pump();
    });
  }
  pump();
});
```

eva: That looks solid. Please add a unit test that simulates partial chunks and ensures no throw + single final event delivered.

maria: Will do. Also note: we should document the server-side headers recommended for customers running proxies in front of Redwood Private: `Cache-Control: no-transform`, `X-Accel-Buffering: no` for nginx, and examples for Envoy config to disable response buffering.

sam: I'll add SDK options and tests. I'll also add logic to dedupe events based on `id` fields so reconnects don't double-process.

ci-bot: maria pushed 0012-sse-client-fallback.patch to branch devex/sse-fixes (CI: running)

josh: Nice. After CI green, let's roll this to staging and run the same stress harness. If staging is good for 2 hours we can ship to prod config update with nginx flags.

maria: Action items: maria PR for docs/tests, sam SDK streaming parser + options, josh run staging + nginx config, eva extend harness. I'll track in #devex-sse board. Thanks :thumbsup:
